WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
Fear of missing out is an anxiety&nbsp;
of losing what we never even had,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.920
and could hence be described as an insane&nbsp;
mental defect. However, it is common,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.500
real, and can make us feel deep regret for&nbsp;
something that hasn’t actually happened.

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:26.760
FOMO is an animal that shies away&nbsp;
from strangers and larger groups,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:30.540
but loves to hide within&nbsp;
intimate social relationships.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.040
It can attack your ego when a close&nbsp;
friend is getting ahead. It tears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:41.940
you down when others are having a&nbsp;
good time and you aren’t invited.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:47.940
It beats you up, if your rival&nbsp;
gets what ought to be yours.&nbsp;

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.240
And if you are lovesick and can’t fall asleep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.900
it’s your FOMO that will give&nbsp;
you the worst nightmares.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:01:01.920
FOMO’s full destructive force&nbsp;
can be observed in Tom and Jeff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:08.160
two friends who decided to invest their&nbsp;
savings into bitcoin, back in 2012.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:16.500
Over the next few years the price of bitcoin went&nbsp;
up a lot, and the two began to believe that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:21.840
were brilliant investors, and that their bet&nbsp;
was the smartest move in their young lives.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:29.940
But they also became increasingly concerned that&nbsp;
something might happen to that precious stake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.940 --> 00:01:35.520
and this new identity of theirs.&nbsp;
Tom experienced fear of loss.

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:44.280
Then, one day, the price of bitcoin suddenly&nbsp;
started falling and then dropped dramatically.

00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:52.380
Tom's fear of loss became too strong and&nbsp;
he decided to sell half of his coins.

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:55.620
Jeff, a religious believer, didn’t sell.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:00.900
After he cashed out, Tom felt relieved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:05.400
even though his remaining bitcoin&nbsp;
investment kept falling further.

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:11.520
We see this happen in Tom, because&nbsp;
while he might still lose money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:16.800
he wants to protect what has become&nbsp;
even more precious to him— his identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:22.560
of being that brilliant investor.
But then our story takes a dark twist.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.500
A short while later, the price of Bitcoin first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:32.280
stabilized and then kept&nbsp;
rising — faster and faster.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:40.380
But Tom, who still held plenty of&nbsp;
coins, wasn’t happy. No. While he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.960
lay in bed at night his FOMO crept out of&nbsp;
hiding and painted a terrifying future.

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:52.800
Tom began to believe that he had made&nbsp;
a massive mistake that would leave him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:58.080
a poor fool, while his friend would be the&nbsp;
millionaire they both dreamed of becoming.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:04.980
And so, even though he still owned&nbsp;
a significant amount of bitcoin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:11.340
he now hoped for a total crash in its value,&nbsp;
and the absolute failure of cryptocurrencies.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:13.620
So, what’s going on here?

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:23.160
The FOMO attacks Tom, because Jeff is his&nbsp;
friend. Same age. Same tribe. Someone just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:29.520
like him. The difference is that while Jeff&nbsp;
is a brilliant investor, Tom is an idiot.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:37.500
And because Tom knows he could have achieved&nbsp;
Jeff’s success, and doesn’t want to live with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:42.240
that idea for the rest of his life,&nbsp;
he hopes they’ll both fail miserably.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:49.920
Research on FOMO suggests:
There are no gender differences&nbsp;

00:03:49.920 --> 00:03:53.940
Higher FOMO scores are associated with younger age&nbsp;

00:03:53.940 --> 00:04:01.200
It’s associated with people who are neurotic
And those who are lonely experience it more often

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:08.220
But which fear is stronger? The&nbsp;
fear of losing what is ours or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:12.960
our fear of missing out? Or are they&nbsp;
even, essentially, the same thing?

